Argument in Favor of Measure E
82% of OC cities have term limits for their City Councils. As it turns 100
years old, the City of Laguna Beach is one of the few that doesn’t.
The power of incumbency is immense. It often results in entrenched
politicians who become less responsive to voters and more beholden to
special interests.
Running against an incumbent is a big deterrent for newcomers, as
incumbents have the advantage of inside information, free media
exposure, staff support, name recognition, and an established political
base with donors in place. The pattern is indisputable; new comers have
to spend significantly more than incumbents in order to win a seat. Term
limits helps level the playing field.
Term limits ensure that no one person can amass enough political power
to effectively control the city. Term limits also enliven our democracy by
bringing more people with fresh new ideas onto council.
Term Limits would add a common sense restriction on who can stand for
election to council, similar to requirements of minimum age, citizenship,
and city residence.
If passed, starting after the 2026 general election, council members
will be limited to two sequential four-year council terms. They would be
eligible to run again after a two-year hiatus. Councilpersons who term-out needn’t disappear; they can continue contributing to civic life through
advisory roles, appointment to commissions or boards, or other forms of
public service before becoming eligible to run again for council.
With Laguna’s staggered election cycle, there will always be at least two
members remaining on the Council to help ensure there’s a continuity of
experience and governance.
We urge you to vote YES for this common sense tool to reduce the
power of special interests, broaden the pool of candidates, and
revitalize Laguna’s governance.
s/ Michael E. Morris
Treasurer, Nimblegov PAC
