Rebuttal to Argument in Favor of Measure E
Laguna Beach works. Our coastline is protected, our neighborhoods are
walkable, and our community events still feel like community events.
People choose to live here because this city is governed well and held
accountable. Laguna voters turn out at over 80%, one of the highest rates
in Orange County.
Now a small group wants to change the rules, not because the system
is broken, but because they keep losing under it. The same people who
pushed a rejected ballot measure in 2022 and failed to win a council seat
in 2024 are back, this time asking voters to surrender the right to reelect
the leaders they trust.
The record does not support their case. Since 1992, 76% of Laguna Beach
council members served two four-year terms or less. Only two have ever
served four consecutive terms. Today, three of five council members are
serving their first terms. In our last election, a fourth-term incumbent won
more votes than any other candidate on the ballot, because the voters
chose him.
A UC San Diego study of all 50 states found that when experienced
officials are forced out by a schedule, power does not shift to the public.
It shifts to lobbyists, unelected staff, and special interests who never have
to face a ballot.
Elections are the real accountability tool. Laguna uses them exceptionally
well.
What we have is working. Do not let a small group repeatedly rejected by
voters rewrite the rules for the rest of us.
s/ Chris Quilter
s/ Rebecca Visconti
s/ Joe Hanauer
s/ Joy Dittberner
s/ S. Cody Engle
