Argument Against Measure E
Does Laguna need term limits? No. Will we be better off with them? No!
Is there a need? The record shows Laguna voters are among the most
civically engaged in Orange County, with turnout typically over 80%.
Council members win elections because they’re good and constituents
trust them— not because the system is broken. Since 1992, 76% of city
councilmembersservedtwotermsorfewer; fourservedthreeconsecutive
terms, and just two served four. In our last election, a councilmember won
his fourth term with more votes than any other candidate. Laguna Beach
city council has 3 fresh faces serving their first term, two elected in 2022
and one in 2024. In the last four elections, four incumbents were voted
out, losing their seats to new council members. The system already turns
over.
Will Laguna be better off with limits? No! They don’t create a
“wider range” of candidates — they force out experienced officials on
an arbitrary clock. That hands power to unelected staff and insiders
while newly elected members are still learning the ropes. Laguna faces
complex, long-term issues that benefit from experienced leadership.
Equating tenure with corruption insults the officials and the voters who
chose them.
Termlimitsdon’taddaccountability—electionsaretherealaccountability
mechanism. Voters should be able to choose who they want without
artificial barriers. Tenure doesn’t guarantee competence, but term limits
guarantee the opposite: a less experienced council plus voters stripped of
the right to reelect leaders they still trust.
Proponents of term limits ask you to trade your right to vote for candidates
you trust. Don’t let them. This same group sponsored a losing 2022 ballot
measure. They then lost a 2024 council bid. Don’t surrender your right to
choose because they lost under the current system.
Protect your right to vote. No on term limits.
