Rebuttal to Argument Against Measure E
Laguna Beach is a unique city, but our system of government is no
different from that of any other city. While we face complex challenges,
keeping councilmembers in office for many years is not the solution. Over
time, some long-serving officials run out of new ideas while others lose
touch with the residents they were elected to represent.
We currently have one councilmember who will have served 16 years by
the end of their term and another running for their 3rd term. Despite
significant resident opposition, they continue to strongly advocate for the
purchase of Laguna Canyon Road. Are they still listening to the people
they represent? Did the decision to remove the mature trees on Forest
Avenue reflect the wishes of the community?
Opponents of term limits argue that we would lose valuable experience
on the City Council. That argument overlooks an important fact:
councilmembers can continue serving Laguna in many meaningful ways
after completing two four-year terms and they may run again after a two-year break. In addition, Laguna’s staggered election cycle ensures that
experienced councilmembers remain on the dais to provide continuity
and institutional knowledge. Our initiative preserves the opportunity for
respected former councilmembers to return while giving qualified new
candidates a fair chance to serve.
Incumbency is one of the greatest advantages any elected official has in
seeking re-election. This initiative creates a thoughtful balance between
preserving experience and opening the door to new leadership, fresh
ideas, and greater accountability.
Please vote YES for term limits.
